  {
    "episode_id": 72711,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72711.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "I think we got the funding for it, but it's not a fully functioning person. There was actually someone in the Department of Agriculture, I think back in the Clinton administration that just worked on food waste. And again, it's another interesting topic when I first heard about the problems with food waste in this country. I mean, people suggest we waste about a third of the food in this country. And this is even before the pandemic and the falling apart of our food system. That's a wake-up call. We're losing it in the field, we're losing it in restaurants and how people buy at home. Again, I think we have a little different lens right now because of this, but everybody understands we shouldn't be wasting food. Grandmother told you not to waste food, and you realize the economic impact on it, the fact that you know we're using up our water supply growing the food and some of it's not getting to hungry people and again there's there's a lot more interested in it now because now people are seeing wholesale uh... loss of food you know farmers in florida plowing under acres of you know onions carrots you know all kinds of things bell peppers everything else so i think we're going to come out of this with a lot more interest and we're certainly gonna you know pay attention during the appropriations process this year but also I see a lot of these issues that will really be tackled now for the next Farm Bill, and that's our best chance to look at the system and say, here's the Farm Bill, it covers everything we've got here, and where does food waste fit in? Where do our problems with our supply chain cause even more waste?",
