  {
    "episode_id": 72711,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72711.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, thank you. Well, first off, thank you so much. I really You know, as you can see, I'm passionate about these issues and I may not always be right, but I just love to think about it and talk about them and I can't tell you how lucky I am to be a member of Congress and get the opportunity to work on these things. And I guess if I were just saying closing, you know, there are those moments in time when there are real opportunities to rethink a system, to make substantive change, and to do it in a way that, you know, brings kind of everybody together around an issue. And I hope that Congress tackles this. As we move forward, this pandemic in particular has really shown us that we have such a bifurcated food system. Some food goes to consumers and some goes into the food service industry. And when you have a complete collapse of the food service industry, when you're shutting down your schools and your hospitals are under capacity, your restaurants are closed down, all of a sudden there is food with nowhere to go. And then people who are hungry because they can't get what they need. And it just provides us with that moment in time to say, OK, we've got to dig in here. Our country has been challenged in a lot of ways. And that is now laid bare. And we've got to look at all of our problems. What have we done to farmers with our trade policy? What's happened to farmers who are suffering with low commodity prices or overproduction? Anyway, it's a great moment in time. I consider myself incredibly lucky. to be able to be part of this dialogue. And I know that I'm going to have a lot of colleagues from all persuasions from all around the country who want to talk about the same things. So here's hoping we can make some real changes and move forward.",
